<Layout
  title="What does it really mean to rechart your life?"
  description="Reinvention isn't a defined concept in psychology. What researchers talk about instead is more interesting — and a lot kinder."
>
  <article style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:30px 8px 90px">
    <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-muted);margin:0 0 10px">17 November 2026</p>
    <h1 style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-weight:500;font-size:clamp(32px,4vw,46px);line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 34px">What does it really mean to rechart your life?</h1>

    <div style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.75;color:var(--color-text-muted)">
      <p>There&rsquo;s a word lots of people misunderstand, even though we use it all the time (and I&rsquo;d say satnavs get it a little bit wrong too).</p>
      <p>Recharting. Or, as my satnav would put it: <em>recalculating.</em></p>
      <p>No wonder that when people hear it, a very specific picture pops into their head: you take a big eraser, rub out your life so far, and start being someone else. New job, new city, new haircut. A new trip, a few new skills, and I won&rsquo;t hold it against you if somewhere at the back of your mind there&rsquo;s a pottery class too, just in case. (Been there &#x1F60E;)</p>
      <p>But let me offer you a twist. The research &ndash; which I&rsquo;ve been digging through quite a lot these past few weeks, half out of a researcher&rsquo;s curiosity, and the other half because it&rsquo;s very much on my mind right now &ndash; says something quite different.</p>
      <p>It turns out that &ldquo;reinvention&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t a neatly defined concept in psychology at all. Researchers work with words like <em>transition</em>, <em>identity work</em>, <em>narrative identity</em>. What they have in common will surprise no one: every so often there comes a point in your life when the way you&rsquo;ve always understood yourself no longer answers a fairly simple question.</p>
      <p style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-weight:500;font-style:italic;font-size:24px;color:var(--color-text);margin:30px 0">Who am I in this life that&rsquo;s happening right now?</p>
      <p>And that point, of course, is rarely a single event. Not necessarily a divorce or being made redundant or moving house (although it can be).</p>
      <p>Very often it&rsquo;s simply that what you&rsquo;ve built suddenly doesn&rsquo;t sit on you the way it used to. First just one small thing doesn&rsquo;t fit. Then another, and then, in no time at all, the whole shop&rsquo;s upside down, because it&rsquo;s coming at you from every direction.</p>
      <p>One of the oldest findings in career research is that a transition is not an event but a process &ndash; and you&rsquo;re often well into it long before you even notice.</p>
      <p>Then there&rsquo;s the question of identity, which for me is the most exciting part. For a long time we were lulled into believing (or lulled ourselves into believing) that adult identity gets built once and then it&rsquo;s done, like a three-door BRIMNES wardrobe, where there are always four mysterious screws left over at the end, and it&rsquo;s best not to know what they were for.</p>
      <p>Whereas, whoops, it keeps taking shape all through adulthood. There&rsquo;s continuity and change in you at the same time &ndash; you want to keep the feeling that you&rsquo;re the same person, while life keeps bringing new roles, losses and possibilities.</p>
      <p>So that&rsquo;s why my eye twitches a little at &ldquo;find your new self&rdquo; type advice. Recharting is very rarely about manufacturing a new you. It&rsquo;s much more about building a new, closer and, not least, accepting relationship with what&rsquo;s already there in you right now.</p>
      <p>A previous profession, for example, wasn&rsquo;t just a job. It held skills, relationships, ways of thinking, problems you loved solving &ndash; and some you never want to see again. When you change career, you&rsquo;re not starting from zero. You bring your whole previous life with you, whether you want to or not. Feel free to swap in a previous relationship, or a thousand other things that shape a life.</p>
      <p>And to make it a bit more personal, because although there&rsquo;s no guaranteed stage of life when this hits us, well: midlife is especially fertile ground. Not because you&rsquo;re obliged to have a crisis somewhere around forty or fifty, but because by then there&rsquo;s usually plenty to reinterpret.</p>
      <p>There&rsquo;s a past, there&rsquo;s a professional identity, there are decisions that can&rsquo;t be undone. And at the same time there&rsquo;s a future too. The two together make for a rather special situation.</p>
      <p>Maybe that&rsquo;s exactly why it&rsquo;s so hard to notice when it arrives. Because there&rsquo;s no official notification. You don&rsquo;t get a push message on your phone saying &ldquo;support for your current identity will end soon, please update&rdquo;. Usually all you feel is that something that used to work doesn&rsquo;t quite work any more.</p>
      <p>Which, you know, isn&rsquo;t necessarily a bad thing. Maybe your life has simply got to the point where the old map no longer shows every road.</p>
      <p>So this is what I&rsquo;ve worked out: life tosses you about, you fall, you get up, you create, you knock things down &ndash; and if you&rsquo;ve outgrown your skin, you don&rsquo;t have to become a completely new person, because you&rsquo;re already pretty great as you are&hellip;</p>
      <p>Instead, skilfully and with a gentle hand, you start reading your own story differently. The old failure doesn&rsquo;t simply stay a failure. An old job isn&rsquo;t ten wasted years. An interest you dropped isn&rsquo;t just &ldquo;something you used to like&rdquo;.</p>
      <p>It&rsquo;s raw material. Data. Ingredients &ndash; and if so far you&rsquo;ve been making, say, a nice lecs&oacute; with them (a pepper-and-tomato stew from the country I grew up in, the kind every family swears they make best), now you could make ANYTHING, just as delicious, or even better.</p>
      <p>Think about it &#x1F60A; The same memories. The same experiences. Only the connections between them will be different.</p>
    </div>

